==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem|ang}} *{{term||īsarna-}}, probably from {{etyl|cel|ang}} *{{term||īsarno-}}.  Cognates include Old Saxon {{term|isarn|īsarn|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|ijzer|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|isarn|īsarn|lang=goh}} (German {{term|Eisen|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|isarn|ísarn|lang=non}}, {{term|járn|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|jern|lang=da}} or {{term|jærn|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|järn|lang=se}}), Gothic {{term|𐌴𐌹𐍃𐌰𐍂𐌽||tr=eisarn|lang=got}}, Old Irish {{term|īärn|lang=sga}} (Irish {{term|iarann|lang=ga}}, Welsh {{term|haearn|lang=cy}}). The Celtic source was related to Latin {{term|aes||bronze|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/'i:zern/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
'''īsern''' ''n'' (''pl'' '''īsern''')

# the metal [[iron]]
# an instrument or tool made from iron, especially an iron [[sword]]
# an iron [[shackle]] or [[fetter]]

===Adjective===
'''īsern'''

# consisting or made of [[iron]]

